WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:05.930
Few mythical beasts loom larger in the human
imagination than the dragon.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:11.060
No matter what form these scaly creatures
take or which folklore they come from, legends

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:15.809
are almost always more captivating when they
include these fantastical monsters.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:20.200
But what might the biology of a ‘realistic’
order of dragons look like?

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.311
Classic books like Dragonology and fictional
documentaries like Discovery Channel’s Dragon

00:00:25.311 --> 00:00:29.190
Special have entertained this hypothetical
idea in the past.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.550
I won’t deny I read and watched both these
series when I was younger more times than

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:37.739
I can count… but I’ve always been looking
for more scientific takes on these mythical

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:38.870
creatures.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.760
Enter Draconology — a meticulously-detailed
spin on the concept that imagines dragons

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.670
as a parareptilian offshoot which emerged
millions of years ago, and have survived until

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.050
the modern day.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.309
The project was created by the artist VikasRao,
and I have links to his work in this video’s

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:56.309
description.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:02.709
His Draconology series is unbelievably comprehensive,
imagining the genetics, behavior, taxonomy,

00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:08.560
habitats, and even feeding habits of countless
dragons inspired by cultures the world over.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.560
So, for this entry into the archive, let’s
make our own dragon documentary, and explore

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.229
the awesome fictional science of Draconology.

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:22.850
The World of Draconology is set on an alternate
Earth, which saw the formation of a few additional

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:28.950
landmasses and the evolution of numerous creatures
that only exist in folklore on our own planet.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.750
Chief among these are the Draconimorpha — a
branch of parareptilia which includes Sea

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:40.450
Serpents, Wyrms, Bird-Dragons like Griffins,
Drakes, Wyverns, and what he calls ‘True

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.590
Dragons’ — all of which are extremely
well-realized, and we’ll be covering in

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:45.929
this video.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:51.369
First up are the sea serpents, or the Thalattophidia,
an order of aquatic draconimorphs that are

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:54.369
a sister lineage to their land-dwelling cousins.

00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:59.189
In the words of the author, the evolutionary
origins of sea serpents are as murky as the

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:04.979
waters they inhabit, but it seems their ancestry
dates back well over two-hundred million years…

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:09.290
and thanks to this alternate earth having
slightly different mass extinctions, some

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.630
still roam the seas today.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.950
In wetlands, rivers, and estuaries throughout
this world’s version of Europe, you may

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.190
encounter a Knucker — a long, eel-like serpent
with sharp, slicing teeth.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.010
Averaging around 16 feet, or five meters in
length, the Knuckers are actually on the small

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.590
side for a sea serpent.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.960
The author imagines their flippers have a
high degree of muscular development, allowing

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.730
them to pull themselves onto land — similar
to seals or sea lions.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:40.720
In mythology, the term ‘Knucker’ was a
common name for a water dragon — with a

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.780
version of the term appearing in the 10th
century epic poem Beowulf.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.330
Moving further out to sea, to the waters of
the Northern Atlantic and Pacific, the Northern

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.180
Sea Orm glides through the waters.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.311
Reaching almost sixty feet, or 18 meters in
length and weighing well over a ton, these

00:02:57.311 --> 00:03:01.420
creatures aren’t built for speed, but for
foraging in the ocean depths.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.900
The long tendrils on its snout, known as barbels,
contain nerve endings that help Sea Orms detect

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.410
vibrations made by swimming prey.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:14.810
The animal’s head is also somewhat crocodilian,
and has a similarly formidable biteforce that

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.590
aids in crushing its unlucky prey.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.640
And in the depths of the ocean, leviathans
lurk.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:27.510
The two largest sea serpents are the Tiamat
and the Jömungandr — examples of abyssal

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:31.920
giantism that get their names from ancient
Mesopotamian and ancient Norse mythology,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.920
respectively.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.760
And these legendary titles are well deserved,
for the largest of the Jömungandr have reached

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:44.640
lengths of 82 feet, or 25 meters — with
the largest Tiamat not far behind at 68 feet,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.040
or 21 meters.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.241
The author imagines these species are able
to withstand the frigid depths thanks to a

00:03:51.241 --> 00:03:55.840
network of heat exchangers and a thick layer
of blubber, which has allowed them to dive

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.850
deep enough to feed on organisms like giant
squids.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.950
In our next category we have the Wyrms — long,
wingless, quadrupedal draconimorphs.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:09.680
In many real-world cultures, dragon myths
don’t feature winged, fire-breathing monsters

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:15.590
but serpentine beings often associated with
water or earth: which this group takes inspiration

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:16.590
from.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:21.830
In the forests of East and Southeast Asia,
the wide-ranging Great Wind Wyrm move through

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.000
the canopy.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.110
Although they lack wings, this species is
able to glide from tree branch to tree branch

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.770
by flaring out its ribs and undulating its
body in a serpentine motion.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:37.770
Such behavior is not dissimilar from the rib-powered
flight of real-life Draco Lizards or the incredible

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:42.520
gliding techniques of paradise tree snakes
— both of which are incredible animals I’ve

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.330
touched on before in other entries.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:49.890
Another amazing creature dwells in various
regions of North America — the Greater Banded

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:50.890
Uktena.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:56.080
At 14 feet, or 4.5 meters, it’s one of the
mightiest species in its family.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.590
The Uktena pays respect to the horned serpent
motif that appears in the oral history of

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.570
numerous indigenous cultures — in particular
the Cherokee people.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:09.540
Able to produce venom from a specialized gland
in its upper jaw, the Uktena is similar to

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.370
a real-world horned viper: one of the deadliest
snakes alive today.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.920
Our third category are the amazing ‘bird
dragons,’ or Orinthodraconia.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.000
This diverse order includes numerous mythical
creatures you might be familiar with.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.760
In West Asia, North Africa, and Southern Europe,
for example, you can find the Common Cockatrice

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.140
— a unique, opportunistic forager that will
eat just about anything.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.920
In conventional mythology, cockatrices hatch
from a chicken egg that was incubated by a

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:40.920
toad.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.080
Here, the author imagines the myth comes from
a behavioral quirk of these reptiles to lay

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.840
their eggs in burrows dug out by certain species
of burrowing toads or lizards.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.840
Pretty clever!

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.650
A strikingly tall Bird Dragon is the Giant
Kirin, a heavy, flightless draconimorph so

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.200
tall it could look a giraffe in the eye.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.000
These unique, brightly colored organisms are
actually inspired by mythical chimerical creatures

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.490
in various East Asian cultures, and have gone
by numerous similar names.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:11.930
And the final member of the Bird Dragons are
the Griffins — a family so diverse they

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.320
require their own separate section.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.790
Called the Gryphonidae, these offshoots have
a superficial resemblance to birds, but their

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:24.170
‘feathers’ are in fact epidermal scales
that have evolved into fur-like Coelofibers.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:28.500
In the forests of Atlantis — another fictional
island landmass — you need to stay on the

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.290
lookout for the Atlantean Giant Alke.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:35.080
These species are the heaviest of the Griffin
family, to the point where they’ve given

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.050
up the ability to fly.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:41.830
Giant Alke are also hypocarnivorous, meaning
their diet is mostly comprised of plants rather

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.820
than meat — similar to many species of bears.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.920
Soaring over the ocean with a wingspan similar
to that of an albatross, the Southern Axex

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.920
is one of the smallest members of the Griffin
family.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.850
These creatures are opportunistic scavengers,
primarily feeding on carcasses and filling

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.490
an ecological niche, almost like a sea-going
vulture.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:06.360
In this scene from the North Pacific, a Northern
Axex — a close relative of their Southern

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.760
cousins — feeds on the washed-up body of
a Right Whale… which a Great White Shark

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.570
has also laid claim to.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:21.380
And the final bird-dragon we’ll be examining
is the Lemurian Giant Axex — the largest

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:25.690
gryphon species, though not quite as heavy
as the Atlantean Alke.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:31.710
Standing over six-and-half feet, or two meters
tall, the Atlantean Alke is a Griffin that

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:36.199
feeds in shallow waters, with a feeding ecology
similar to storks or herons — although the

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:37.199
Alke reaches far greater sizes.

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:40.740
In our next category, we have the drakes,
or Therosuchia.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:45.770
These heavyset, flightless draconimorphs are
closely related to what the artist terms ‘true

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.630
dragons’ (which I’ll get to in a bit).

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:53.260
The author imagines most Drakes are semi-aquatic,
growing to gargantuan sizes feeding on vast

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.790
schools of fish.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:08:00.130
The largest species, the Titan Drake, is a
giant among giants, reaching lengths of up

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:06.610
to 52 feet, or 16 meters and weighing over
9 tons — making them proportional in size

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.260
to a T-rex.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:13.970
Out to sea, the swift Makara has become even
more aquatic, growing a long tail fluke to

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:17.810
aid in pursuing prey under the waves in coordinated
pods.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:23.071
On occasion, makara will even attempt to catch
their distant cousins, the sea serpents, as

00:08:23.071 --> 00:08:26.520
you can see here in this high-speed underwater
chase.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.199
While the Makara are fast, most species of
sea serpents have better endurance — making

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:33.010
this a tight race for survival.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:40.089
And now we’ve arrived at an amazing category
of flying draconiformes, the Wyverns, or Volanosauria,

00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:45.680
an order that is famous not for breathing
fire but for being venomous.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.339
At first glance, these flying reptiles seem
to resemble our next category of true dragons

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:54.759
more than the Drakes or Bird Dragons, but
they’re actually more closely related to

00:08:54.759 --> 00:08:56.269
certain Wyrms.

00:08:56.269 --> 00:09:02.199
On various island chains, the Greater Frogmouth
Wyvern flits among the trees, feeding on small

00:09:02.199 --> 00:09:03.910
birds and reptiles.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:08.660
Although capable of flight, Frogmouth Wyverns’
wings, like the wings of most Wyverns, lack

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:14.220
the air-sacs that allow true dragons to achieve
lift off despite their far greater sizes.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:19.660
As a result, Frogmouth Wyverns have stayed
relatively tiny, not much larger than a crow.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:24.990
And no, despite their name, frogmouth wyverns
aren’t imagined as being related to actual

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:25.990
frogs.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:30.990
In the Amazon rainforest, the much larger
Peacock Wyverns also achieve flight, with

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:35.269
wingspans of over five feet, or 1.5 meters.

00:09:35.269 --> 00:09:39.880
One notable trait of this species is their
lethal venom glands, which produce a deadly

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.860
fast-acting neurotoxin.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:48.060
Peacock Wyverns also possess bright, flashy
colors much like real-world peacocks to attract

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.060
mates.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.230
And the largest Wyvern in this fictional taxonomy
is the New Guinean Giant Wyvern, apex predators

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:58.629
whose wingspans get close to ten feet, or
3 meters in length.

00:09:58.629 --> 00:10:03.999
Like most Wyverns, these creatures also possess
potent venom, enabling them to hunt animals

00:10:03.999 --> 00:10:06.470
many times their size.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:11.889
Impressive as these creatures are, however,
true dragons can grow far larger…

00:10:11.889 --> 00:10:16.389
Before we discuss the category of what the
artist calls true dragons, however, I need

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:20.800
address a misconception I’ve often seen
online regarding the difference between Wyverns

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:24.790
and Dragons that I might be… a bit too passionate
about.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:30.109
I’ve often heard people claim that any Dragon
with two legs is automatically a Wyvern, which

00:10:30.109 --> 00:10:34.399
— okay, obviously both dragons and wyverns
aren’t real, so who cares.

00:10:34.399 --> 00:10:39.379
But if we’re going off mythology as a precedent,
this division is far from universal.

00:10:39.379 --> 00:10:44.130
The numbers of limbs ‘dragons’ in medieval
manuscripts have are all over the place — despite

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:49.119
the misconception that dragons always had
four legs and two wings and wyverns always

00:10:49.119 --> 00:10:50.939
had two legs and two wings.

00:10:50.939 --> 00:10:56.339
Though Wyverns usually had two legs, Dragons
in myth often had any number of legs, and

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:59.940
yet could still be identified as ‘dragons.’

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:05.420
If myth is your basis, I think a better distinction
is that Wyverns, unlike Dragons, usually couldn’t

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.709
breathe fire.

00:11:07.709 --> 00:11:12.790
The notion of a hard division between dragons
and wyverns based on legs alone is a very

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:15.139
recent and subjective one.

00:11:15.139 --> 00:11:17.879
So, if you want your dragons to have four
legs, awesome!

00:11:17.879 --> 00:11:21.829
But if you want them to have two, that doesn’t
‘automatically’ make them wyverns.

00:11:21.829 --> 00:11:25.189
And again, all of this is made up.

00:11:25.189 --> 00:11:27.940
Okay, back on track…

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:33.379
At last, we’ve come to the fire-breathing
Eudraconia, called ‘true dragons’ in this

00:11:33.379 --> 00:11:34.379
taxonomy.

00:11:34.379 --> 00:11:37.490
(Who you’ll notice have two legs, and yet
aren’t wyverns.)

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:41.940
Among the ranks of these dragons are the large,
fire-breathing behemoths you’d expect, but

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:45.149
also smaller, more specialized species.

00:11:45.149 --> 00:11:50.769
The Green Dragonet, for example, is a diminutive
dragon that, isolated on the Solomon Islands,

00:11:50.769 --> 00:11:54.360
had adopted a curious diet of raw fruits.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:59.129
Despite being smaller than some wyverns, the
Green Dragonet still counts as a true dragon

00:11:59.129 --> 00:12:03.679
thanks to the brachial air sacs on its wing…
and because it retains the fire-breathing

00:12:03.679 --> 00:12:05.970
abilities of its larger cousins.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:11.209
Near the rivers and wetlands of the fictional
landmass of Lemuria, the gargantuan Lemurian

00:12:11.209 --> 00:12:13.600
Dracolisk is on the prowl.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:20.079
Despite having a wingspan of 44 feet, or 13.5
meters, the Dracolisk spends much of its time

00:12:20.079 --> 00:12:24.439
patrolling the water’s edge looking for
food, and has actually taken up an ecological

00:12:24.439 --> 00:12:29.519
niche not unlike a hippopotamus — if hippos
were carnivores and could fly and breathe

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:30.639
fire, that is.

00:12:30.639 --> 00:12:33.860
But how do the dragons of this world breathe
fire?

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:40.410
The author imagines the process begins internally,
starting by combining endogenously-produced

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:45.009
pyrooric compounds in a specialized “reaction
chamber” located in the throat.

00:12:45.009 --> 00:12:50.540
The creatures then forcibly expel this mixture
outwards, producing a powerful jet of flame

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.940
— almost like a biological flamethrower.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:58.779
The most impressive fire-breather of all is
the Eurasian Mountain Devil, a megafaunal

00:12:58.779 --> 00:13:03.670
predator with a wingspan of almost 50 feet,
or 15 meters.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:09.179
These beasts hunt by divebombing prey from
above, ending the hunt with a quick burst

00:13:09.179 --> 00:13:10.179
of fire.

00:13:10.179 --> 00:13:14.629
In build, the Eurasian Mountain Devil almost
reminds me of some modern reconstructions

00:13:14.629 --> 00:13:20.339
of pterosaurs, although this dragon is larger
than even the mightiest of that extinct group.

00:13:20.339 --> 00:13:25.119
In the skies, the Mountain Devils are unmatched
— as you can see one here scaring off two

00:13:25.119 --> 00:13:27.850
Griffin with a jet of ominous flame.

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.680
A truly intimidating creature…

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:35.519
You can find more species and scientific details
on VikasRao’s site using the links below.

00:13:35.519 --> 00:13:40.660
I’d like to give a special shout out to
Ernesto Marrero, who, I believe, was my nineteenth

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:45.319
subscriber, and the first person ever to give
me a suggestion for a video, back when I was

00:13:45.319 --> 00:13:48.079
averaging less than ten views an entry.

00:13:48.079 --> 00:13:52.410
His request was for a video on dragons, and
I feel it’s only right I finally delivered!

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:56.629
So, thank you Ernesto — it’s because of
you and fans like you that I make these videos

00:13:56.629 --> 00:13:58.049
in the first place!

00:13:58.049 --> 00:13:59.929
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:13:59.929 --> 00:14:04.610
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:07.369
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:14:07.369 --> 00:14:20.179
See you in the next video.

